NAME
    tmux - multiple independent shell sessions

SYNOPSIS
    tmux new [name]
    tmux list
    tmux switch <name>
    tmux kill <name>

DESCRIPTION
    Each session is a fully independent DianaShInterpreter - its own
    variables, command history, and $?/pending-multiline-input state -
    that you can create and switch between. 'new' with no name auto-numbers
    it (session-1, session-2, ...). All sessions observe the same one live
    core/ROM, the same way several real tmux windows attached to one real
    machine each get their own independent shell but see the same one
    machine - this is NOT multiple games running at once, just multiple
    independent shells watching the one that is.

    Because a DianaShInterpreter can't be repointed at a new target once
    built (see 'core'), swapping the ROM rebuilds every session's
    interpreter from scratch, the same 'shell-level state resets on reload'
    convention 'core' already documents - it's not just the current session
    that loses its variables/history on a ROM swap, all of them do.

    Two things deliberately SURVIVE that rebuild: a session's name, and the
    account it is running as (see 'su'). The account is carried forward
    explicitly rather than left to reset, because a ROM swap dropping every
    session back to root would be a silent privilege escalation the moment a
    permission system actually enforces anything. For the same reason a new
    session inherits whoever created it rather than starting as root, so
    'tmux new' can never be a way back to an account you left.

    Not available everywhere: a host needs to opt in by handing
    DianaShInterpreter.CreateDefault a DianaShSessionManager. Where it IS
    available, whether switching sessions is itself safe against a
    concurrently-running emulation thread is that host's own concern, not
    this command's - EmuSen.Hotaru/EmuSen.Mistress each keep a separate
    DianaShInterpreterScheduler per session for exactly that reason; the
    standalone DianaSh shell (no core, no concurrent thread) needs none at
    all.

EXAMPLES
    tmux new
    tmux new investigation
    tmux list
    tmux switch investigation
    tmux kill session-1
